  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $7-6\\text{i}=(4p-3q\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $1+2\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-6\\text{i} & =(4p-3q\\text{i})(1+2\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{7-6\\text{i}}{1+2\\text{i}} & =4p-3q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(7-6\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})}{(1+2\\text{i})(1-2\\text{i})} & =4p-3q\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{7-14\\text{i}-6\\text{i}+12{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} & =4p-3q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{-5-20\\text{i}}{1+4} & =4p-3q\\text{i} \\\\ \\frac{-5-20\\text{i}}{5} & =4p-3q\\text{i} \\\\ -1-4\\text{i} & =4p-3q\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $-1=4p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-4=-3q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{4}{3}.$",
